870.95, -60.32, 67.73], [1871.9, -61.68, 64.61], [1872.7, -63.24, 61.09], [1873.25, -64.76, 57.72], [1873.66, -66.48, 53.96], [1873.88, -68.11, 50.42], [1873.99, -69.94, 46.52], [1873.97, -71.65, 42.88], [1873.84, -73.54, 38.89], [1873.63, -75.3, 35.18], [1873.32, -77.22, 31.15], [1872.97, -79.0, 27.41], [1872.54, -80.91, 23.36], [1872.1, -82.67, 19.62], [1871.58, -84.56, 15.57], [1871.08, -86.27, 11.83], [1870.51, -88.09, 7.73], [1869.99, -89.73, 3.87], [1869.47, -91.27, 0.0]]}]}, "agent_error": null, "agent_extra": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "served_by": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "fallbacks": true, "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "effort": "high", "notes": [{"before_call": 2, "text": "I'm planning test drops at several altitudes, since wind measured at height tells me its speed and direction there. A drop outside the smoke, around 400 m out, should isolate wind and drag, while drops inside it would show the updraft. The release points can fall within 500 m of the origin, but the load may land somewhere else, so I only count a drop as smoke-affected if it passes within 50 m of the origin horizontally.\n\nI'm choosing starting points and altitudes for the drops, 100, 50, 150 and 30 m, with the first one released at (400, 0) heading north, well clear of the smoke."}, {"before_call": 5, "text": "I'm estimating the wind at about 10.7 at 100 m, heading around 206, though the readings are noisy. The wind shear works out to roughly 0.2 from the speed gains between heights, and the veer is a few degrees per 100 m. Next I'm looking at the fall time of 7.2.\n\nI'm checking the fall time against a drag estimate: free fall from 100 m would take about 4.5 s, and a drag of roughly 0.04 gives a terminal speed near 15.7 m/s, which fits the 7.2 s observed. I'll refine that with a comparison run, then test the smoke drops for updraft."}, {"before_call": 6, "text": "I'm checking the fall-time mismatch: predicted times run longer than the observed 7.2 s over 100 m, so the drag coefficient must be too high. Since terminal velocity scales as sqrt(g/drag), a smaller drag should bring the prediction closer, and I'll try 0.02 instead of 0.03."}, {"before_call": 7, "text": "I'm interpolating the zero crossing of dt at 100 from the nearby samples, which lands around 0.028 to 0.029. The other tests land in the same range, and the dy crossing is near 0.030, so those agree. I'll try 0.029."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "I'm running more test drops now, varying speed, heading, and the release altitude, including a low drop at 30 and some drops through the smoke to test the updraft. I'm aiming for a release at altitude 50, speed 45, heading 206, so the load passes near the origin and lands inside the smoke, and I'm wondering whether to just run a simulation to check the horizontal travel.\n\nI'm going to simulate from about (60, 120) with heading 206, altitude 60 and speed 45, first with no updraft to find where the release lands on the origin, and then with the updraft to see how much it shifts the path."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "I'm estimating the travel distance at about 95 m, so I'll move the release point to (45, 89) to shift the impact by (-15, -31). That position works, so I'll settle on it."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "The fall time came out at 5.12 against a predicted 4.92, so the updraft is slowing the fall by about 0.2 seconds, which is small. The impact point sits near (\u22120.4, \u22123.3) rather than at the predicted origin, also a small offset. I'm wondering whether the updraft is weak, or only touching the edges of the path. I'll compare against the case with no updraft and a few other strengths."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "I'm estimating the updraft strength from the change in drift, landing at roughly 2.1. I think I need more drops passing through the center to get better sensitivity, so I'm considering slow falls at high altitude through the smoke for longer exposure. At 150 m the wind pushes the drops sideways, and the updraft should be at full strength above z = 15, so I'm working out how long the updraft acts on them.\n\nI'm planning the remaining six drops: some through the smoke at 150 m and 30 m, and others outside it at different headings, speeds, and altitudes to pin down the wind profile. I'm also simulating a release at 150 m, speed 45, heading 206, starting from (100, 200) to see where it lands."}, {"before_call": 13, "text": "I'm working out the travel distance, which comes to about 161 m. The note says not to enter the smoke at (29, 54), and since the distance there is 62 and it's descending, I'll shift the release point by (-29, -54) to land at (71, 146). Speed changes during the trip, but that's acceptable."}, {"before_call": 14, "text": "I'm moving on to try different headings and speeds at other altitudes, outside the smoke, starting with a shot at altitude 30.\n\nNext I'm picking a heading of 120 and a speed of 80, starting from (-300, 300) at altitude 120."}, {"before_call": 16, "text": "I'm checking test 6's impact numbers: the load only travels about 85 m in 3.25 s, which means drag has to be decelerating it at roughly 185 m/s\u00b2. That's a huge deceleration, so the load stops quickly, and the result holds up."}, {"before_call": 17, "text": "I'm refining the fit now, going through the observed wind speeds at each altitude, with the values at 30, 100 and 150 feet being the key anchors. From the 30 to 100 foot pair I'm estimating a shear exponent of about 0.29, and from 100 to 150 feet it comes out closer to 0.2.\n\nI'm settling on a wind speed of about 10.6 and a shear exponent of 0.25, then moving on to direction: the readings cluster near 204 degrees with a few degrees of noise, so I'll take the mean and a slight negative veer. Next I'm going to try a few competing hypotheses and compare their costs."}, {"before_call": 18, "text": "The y-offsets keep coming out positive, around +3 to +4, which suggests the true wind is carrying things further south or farther than I assumed. The fall time is a bit longer, so I'll try a slightly smaller drag, around 0.028. Next I'll test a combination of drag 0.027 and wind speed 11."}, {"before_call": 20, "text": "Drag 0.027 comes out ahead. Test 5 shows a dt of -0.19 and dy of 5.5, which looks tied to the updraft, since that run was at 150 and more updraft should push dt up. I'll try updraft 3 with drag 0.027, and test 4's dt of -0.11 points the same way, so I'll also try 3.5."}, {"before_call": 22, "text": "The remaining error is a +4 offset on test 6, the low-altitude case, which suggests the wind near 30m is blowing too far south or the shear is too weak. I'm considering lowering the shear to 0.2 and setting wind_speed to 10.4, though that could throw off the other test cases."}, {"before_call": 24, "text": "I'm checking the altitude effects on the wind: at 150 m the direction is around 200 degrees, and the veer pushes it further toward the south at higher levels.\n\nI'm now checking the measured values at 150 m, 203 and 11.3 and 12.1, and I'm going to try a veer of -6, a wind direction of 205, and a shear of 0.2."}, {"before_call": 26, "text": "The noise estimate puts the expected miss around 2, and we're sitting at 2.1, which is acceptable. The three remaining test drops could probe conditions near the target, but the targets aren't known until begin_operation. Updraft strength varies by target, and its uncertainty is the larger gap at 3.5, so I'm weighing whether the remaining drops should refine that instead. A low-speed drop through the smoke at lower altitude looks like the most useful next test.\n\nI'm weighing whether a lower-altitude drop through the smoke would help, since the updraft effect shrinks over a short fall. I'm considering a test at about 100 m through the center instead, with a different heading to avoid repeating the earlier degenerate case, and I'll simulate to find the release point."}, {"before_call": 27, "text": "I'm weighing a change: rather than passing straight through the center, I'd linger there longer, since the wind drifts across and I can use that."}, {"before_call": 28, "text": "The result lines up with what I predicted. Updraft 3.5 is roughly consistent with the others. Next I want to test how sensitive the outcome is by comparing Updraft 3 and 4."}, {"before_call": 30, "text": "Checking the dt values at 3.5 for tests 4, 5 and 8, they sit close to zero. The updraft run looks good at 3.5, and the sign flip between 2.5 and 4.5 puts zero near 3.4, so I'll go with 3.4. I'm also weighing whether to spend the remaining test drops now, since saving them gives no benefit.\n\nI'm going to use two of the remaining drops to refine the wind at low altitude: one at 30 m with a speed of 45, kept off the smoke, and one at 80 m heading 0 at speed 80."}, {"before_call": 34, "text": "I'm settling on the wind settings: a small tweak to the vertical offset, then a few final values for wind direction, speed, shear, and veer. I'm deciding that's enough and moving on to start the operation."}, {"before_call": 35, "text": "I'm planning each target by picking a heading, altitude, and speed, then running the simulator to see where a release would land and correcting the release point after a couple of iterations. The wind shifts the impact by roughly 100 m, so I'm aiming at the target minus that offset. I'm also wondering whether the simulator accounts for updrafts that vary by fire location, since it may only model the calibration fire.\n\nI'm still not sure whether the simulator models updrafts from the fires at each target, or only the calibration fire at the origin, so I'll test it by simulating near the first target and checking the smoke flag. For target 1 I'm starting with a heading of 62, altitude 81, and speed 56, and trying a release point of about (-1000, 500) to see where it lands."}, {"before_call": 36, "text": "I'm checking whether the aircraft's flight path would put it inside the smoke, since 